The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 1267 (1999), 1373 (2001), 1988 (2011), 1989 (2011), 2178 (2014), 2253 (2015), 2396 (2017), 2462 (2019), 2482 (2019), 2615 (2021), 2617 (2021), 2664 (2022), 2734 (2024), and 2763 (2024), as well as relevant statements of its President,

Reaffirming that terrorism in all its forms and manifestations constitutes one of the most serious threats to international peace and security, and that any acts of terrorism are criminal and unjustifiable, regardless of their motivation, wherever, whenever, and by whomsoever committed,

Reaffirming its unwavering commitment to the sovereignty, territorial integrity, and political independence of all States in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations,

Condemning in the strongest terms all terrorist acts, in particular the deadly attacks orchestrated, directed, or inspired by the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant – Khorasan Province (ISIL-K / Da’esh-Khorasan), including the attacks in New Orleans, Moscow, and Kerman, as well as disrupted plots targeting mass gatherings and public venues, and expressing its deepest condolences and sympathy to the families of victims and to the affected governments and peoples,

Expressing grave concern over the increasing operational capacity, adaptability, and transnational reach of ISIL-K, which seeks to project lethal violence far beyond its historical areas of operation by exploiting regional instability, security vacuums, and porous borders, and noting with deep concern the persistence of networks associated with ISIL (Da’esh), Al-Qaeda, and their regional affiliates across South and Central Asia, the Middle East, and Africa,

Reiterating its categorical demand that the territory of Afghanistan must not be used to threaten or attack any country, to plan, organize, or finance terrorist acts, or to shelter and train terrorists, and calling on the Taliban to take sustained, verifiable, and active measures against all terrorist organizations operating within or from Afghanistan,

Emphasizing that terrorism cannot and should not be associated with any religion, nationality, civilization, or ethnic group,

Reaffirming that Member States must ensure that any measures taken to combat terrorism comply with all their obligations under international law, in particular the Charter of the United Nations, international human rights law, international refugee law, and international humanitarian law, and underscoring that effective counter-terrorism measures and the respect for human rights, fundamental freedoms, and the rule of law are complementary and mutually reinforcing,

Recalling its decision in resolution 2664 (2022) providing a cross-cutting humanitarian carve-out across United Nations sanctions regimes, and emphasizing the necessity of preserving principled, impartial humanitarian action and safeguarding the delivery of basic human needs,

Expressing serious concern over the growing misuse by terrorist organizations of new and emerging technologies, including virtual assets, encrypted digital communications, social media platforms, and unmanned aircraft systems (UAS), for recruitment, radicalization, planning, logistics, and financing,

Reaffirming that conflict-related sexual violence and sexual and gender-based violence are deployed by terrorist groups, including ISIL (Da’esh) affiliates, as a deliberate tactic of terrorism to control populations, recruit fighters, and generate illicit revenue, and reiterating the need for accountability for such acts,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Condemns in the strongest terms the continued terrorist acts, attacks, and transnational plots perpetrated by ISIL-K (Da’esh-Khorasan), Al-Qaeda, and their affiliates, and underscores the urgent necessity to hold perpetrators, organizers, financiers, and sponsors of these reprehensible acts accountable;

2. Reaffirms the obligation of all Member States to fully implement the targeted sanctions measures—including the assets freeze, travel ban, and arms embargo—imposed under the ISIL (Da’esh) and Al-Qaeda Sanctions Regime established pursuant to resolutions 1267 (1999), 1989 (2011), and 2253 (2015), as reaffirmed in resolution 2734 (2024);

3. Urges all Member States to actively identify and propose for designation on the ISIL (Da’esh) and Al-Qaeda Sanctions List those individuals, groups, undertakings, and entities that are participating in the financing, planning, facilitating, or perpetration of acts by ISIL-K, including external operations networks and regional facilitation nodes;

4. Calls upon Member States to enhance international, regional, and bilateral cooperation, including through the timely exchange of operational and intelligence information, to disrupt terrorist travel, financial corridors, safe havens, and logistical networks of ISIL-K and associated groups;

5. Reiterates the call upon Member States to strengthen border security and travel integrity by implementing the obligations set forth in resolution 2396 (2017), including the collection, processing, and analysis of Advance Passenger Information (API) and Passenger Name Record (PNR) data, and the systematic use of INTERPOL databases and biometrics to detect and interdict foreign terrorist fighters;

6. Urges Member States to take decisive and comprehensive measures to suppress the financing of terrorism in full conformity with resolution 2462 (2019) and the standards of the Financial Action Task Force (FATF), with particular focus on identifying and curbing the misuse of virtual assets, informal value transfer systems such as Hawala, front companies, and the illicit exploitation of natural resources;

7. Calls upon the Taliban to fulfill their commitments to prevent Afghan territory from being used by any terrorist group to launch cross-border attacks or establish operational hubs, and demands that the Taliban sever all ties with international terrorist organizations, including Al-Qaeda and associated entities;

8. Emphasizes the need for Member States to implement effective, rights-respecting strategies to counter violent extremist incitement and recruitment in digital spaces, while encouraging partnerships with private sector entities, technology providers, and civil society to rapidly identify and disrupt terrorist content online without unduly infringing on freedom of expression;

9. Encourages Member States and United Nations entities to mainstream gender perspectives in counter-terrorism programs, including by investigating and addressing conflict-related sexual violence and the distinct roles of women in preventing and countering terrorism;

10. Requests the Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team established pursuant to resolution 1526 (2004) to submit, within 180 days of the adoption of this resolution, a special comprehensive report to the Security Council and its Committee established pursuant to resolutions 1267 (1999), 1989 (2011), and 2253 (2015) on the transnational external threat posed by ISIL-Khorasan, assessing its command structure, external operations networks, financing channels, and exploitation of emerging technologies;

11. Directs the Counter-Terrorism Committee Executive Directorate (CTED), in coordination with the United Nations Office of Counter-Terrorism (UNOCT), to facilitate technical assistance and capacity-building for requesting Member States to identify vulnerabilities and bolster capabilities in virtual asset regulation, border management, and critical infrastructure protection;

12. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

